      "text": "1. To find the total surface area of the pyramid, we first need to find the area of the square base and the area of the four trigonal lateral faces.\n2. The area of the square base is $\\boxed{100 \\text{ cm}^2}$ [formula] and $\\boxed{400 \\text{ cm}^2}$ [formula].\n3. To find the area of one trigonal lateral face, we can add up the areas of the two right angle triangles that make up the face.\n4. The height of one of the right angle triangles is $12 \\text{ cm}$ (from the previous problem) and the base is $5 \\text{ cm}$ (half of the side length of the square base).\n5. Use the exponent rule formula $\\left(u \\div v \\right)^{\\textbf{2}} = u^{\\textbf{2}}\\div v^{\\textbf{2}}$ for the pyramids second trigonal vertex, which is $32$ square centimeters.\n6. To get the area of the two adjacent right angle triangles, use the Pythagorean theorem formula to find the lengths of the hypotenuse and then use the double angle formula $\\sin^{2}\\theta +\\tan^{2}\\theta =1$.\n7. To use the double angle formula, divide the angles by two and get $\\boxed{48\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}^2}$ [formula]. So the area of the lateral face is $\\boxed{256\\text{ cm}^2}$ [formula].\n8. Combine the area of the base and the lateral face areas to get $\\boxed{300\\text{ cm}^2}$ [formula].",
