      "text": "We draw a construction drawing to find the lengths of two sides of the pyramid:\n[asy]size(0,200,200);\npair A = (-5,0), B = A+(1,0), C = A+(0.5,sqrt(3)/2), D = A+(-0.5, -sqrt(3)/2);\ndraw(A--D--B--C--A--D);\ndraw((2,0)--(4,0)--(4,4)--(5.6,4)--(5.6,5.2)--(4.8,5.2)--cycle);\ndraw(B--(4,4)--(4,4)--(5.6,4)--cycle);\ndraw((3,0)--(1.7,0)--(1.7,3)--(2,4)--cycle);\nfill((5,0)--(1,0)--(1,sqrt(3)/2)--(2.1,1)--cycle, gray(.9)); draw((1,0)--(1,sqrt(3)/2)--(2.1,1)--cycle);\ndraw(rightanglemark((4,0),A,D,24));\npair O = (2,0), Bi = (3.2,1.6);\ndraw((2.3,0)--(2.7,0)--(5,0)--(5.7,0)--cycle);\ndraw((2.5,0)--Bi);\ndraw((2.75,1)--(3.29,1.58));\ndot((0,0)); label(\"O\",(0,0),W); dot((5.7,0)); label(\"D\",(5.7,0),E);[/asy]\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem on the right triangle formed by the height of the pyramid, half the base edge (10/2=5 cm), and OP (25=12 cm^2+5 cm^2), we obtain O^2 = 100, so the side length of the pyramid is 10 cm.\nSince there are four of these sides, each of length 10 cm, the area of the four sides is 4 \\times 10 \\times 12 = 480 cm^2.\nNext, we determine the area of the two triangular sides of the pyramid.\nIn right triangle EDC (using the slant height of 12 cm as its hypotenuse), half of CD is (10/2)=5 cm. So, the area of one triangle is (1/2)(base)(height) = (1/2)(12 cm)(5 cm) or 30 cm^2.\nSince there are two such triangles, the total area of the two triangles is 30 cm^2 + 30 cm^2 = 60 cm^2.\nFinally, we add the areas of the four sides and the two triangles, which gives us sum = 480 cm^2 + 60 cm^2 = 540 cm^2.\nPutting your reasoning and the final answer in the square brackets, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\boxed{540} square centimeters.",
